    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's a great observation, and indeed one of the complexities in interpreting the novel. While Conrad's critique of imperialism is evident, his portrayal of African characters can indeed be seen as problematic. In the 19th century, European society did often view Africans as \"less than human,\" an idea reflected and perpetuated in literature and societal attitudes of that time. Conrad's narrator, Marlow, when describing African characters, does at times seem to adopt this perspective, though his intentions are more ambiguous.\n\nIt is possible Conrad chose to reflect the language of the time to give the novel authenticity and impact; however, this requires a careful navigation and interpretation today, particularly to avoid overshadowing the novel's critique with its own instance of dehumanization. The critique of imperialism in \"Heart of Darkness\" can function at several levels, including narrative techniques and the use of language. An in-depth academic discussion would consider how these elements work together to imply a condemnation of imperialism, despite the racial insensitivities that present in the text. Still, it's also important to acknowledge that some modern readers find it difficult to overlook the dehumanizing portrayal of African peoples in the novel.\n\nThere are ways to approach this dichotomy while still discussing the novel:",
